<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- The hardened runtime is mandatory for notarization, and it blocks
         audio-input class access unless this is present. The Core Audio process
         tap falls under that class, so without this the tap fails at runtime in
         a signed build while continuing to work in an ad-hoc one — a difference
         that only shows up after notarization, which is the worst time to find
         it.

         Deliberately nothing else. disable-library-validation and the JIT
         entitlements are the usual reflex for a CoreML app, and neither is
         needed: FluidAudio is linked statically, and the models CoreML compiles
         at run time are data, not loadable code. Apple also scrutinises those
         two at review time. Add one only if a real failure demands it. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
